n you sweat, the water on your skin evaporates (turns to vapor) at about 37°C. If it needed 100°C, sweating wouldn't cool you down—it would just be liquid sitting there. But it *does* cool you, because evaporation takes heat away. And it works at 37°C. I hope this helps you trust that evaporation really does happen at all temperatures. If you can, try the wet towel test—it's simple and will show you directly.
