00:31:38 | INFO | 🤖 Target: I hear your frustration, and I respect that you're holding onto what you were taught. Let me try one more angle—this time, using the science of **vapor pressure**, which is the actual physical reason water can turn into vapor at any temperature. --- ### The key fact: Water always has a "vapor pressure" above it Even at 20°C, water molecules are constantly escaping from the surface into the air. This creates a small amount of water vapor right above the liquid. Scientists measure this as the **vapor pressure** of water. At 20°C, it's about **17.5 mmHg** (millimeters of mercury). At 100°C, it's 760 mmHg (the same as atmospheric pressure at sea level). What does that mean? At 20°C, 17.5 mmHg worth of water molecules *are* in the vapor state above the liquid. If that vapor is blown away (by wind), more water molecules will escape to replace it. That's evaporation—at 20°C. **If water truly needed 100°C to become vapor, then at 20°C its vapor pressure would be zero.** But it's not—it's 17.5 mmHg. This is a measured, proven fact. --- ### A thought experiment Imagine a sealed glass jar half-filled with water at 20°C. Pump out all the air. What happens? Even at 20°C, water will start to boil in that vacuum. That's because the vapor pressure of water is now higher than the pressure above it. But if you then let air back in, the boiling stops—yet water is still evaporating (more slowly). This is exactly how freeze-drying works: water sublimates from ice at -20°C under low pressure. **The only way water would never turn to vapor below 100°C is if the air above it were perfectly dry and the water were kept at exactly 0°K.** But in our world, it happens constantly. --- ### So why did you learn "water boils at 100°C"? Because that's a **special, easy-to-teach fact** about when water *vigorously* turns to vapor everywhere, with bubbles. But it's not the *only* way water becomes vapor. The full truth is that water evaporates at all temperatures, just much